        "h2": "What Spreadsheets Do Well and Where They Stop",
        "content": "Spreadsheets earn their popularity honestly. They cost nothing, everyone knows how to use them, and they are endlessly flexible. For a brand-new pressure washing business with one truck and a handful of customers, a spreadsheet can track jobs, log payments, and hold a customer list just fine. There is no reason to buy software before you need it. The trouble is that a spreadsheet is a static record, not a working system. It does not remind a customer about tomorrow's appointment, it does not route a crew efficiently, and it certainly does not charge a card when a job is done. Every one of those actions still has to happen by hand, and the spreadsheet just sits there holding numbers. As long as your volume is tiny, that manual gap is manageable. But the spreadsheet's flexibility is also its weakness: because it does nothing on its own, everything depends on a person remembering to update it correctly, every time. The tool that felt liberating at five customers becomes a fragile stack of manual steps at fifty. Knowing where spreadsheets stop being enough is the first step in deciding whether it is time to switch."
